A brief overview of key terms:

`sap-section`
    A section defined according to the following rules:

        1) If there are no sections before or after the content, the content itself is a sap-section.

        2) If there are no sections before the content, but there are sections after it,
           the sap-section is the content followed by a separator `-` on a separate line.

        3) If there are sections before the content, but no sections after it,
           the sap-section is the content preceded by a separator `-` on a separate line.

        4) If there are sections both before and after the content,
           the sap-section is the content enclosed by separators `-` placed on separate lines.

`->`
    Then.


(1) A BIT ABOUT VERSION NOTATION
=======================================================================================================

(CATPKG 1.X)

    The major version CATPKG 1 defines a format based on divided sap-sections. For example:

`
CATPKG 1.X
package_name
-
__ARCHITECTURE__
-
Description
-
__SHA__
-
package_name1
package_name2
package_name3
-
-
#option1 option text 1
#option1 option text 2
#option2 option text 3
#option3 option text 4
`

    In a CATPKG 1.X specification, each sap-section has its own designation. For example:

        0 - title
        1 - architecture
        2 - description
        3 - sha256 (payload signature)
        4 - dependencies
        5 - options

    Options were added as a standard mechanism for the mandatory declaration of additional values.

    The minor version (CATPKG 1.0, 1.1, 1.2...) defines a specification that determines
    how each primitive and section is designated and interpreted. These specifications
    are described below.


(CATPKG 2.0)

    Version 2.0 is a standard intended to provide a more convenient user experience
    and will be introduced as part of a future release.


(2) CATPKG 1 DSL
=======================================================================================================

CATPKG 1 DSL assumes that when using the catstrap postprocessor and its macros, you should read more
in `catstrap-postprocess` (./catstrap-postprocess).

(2.1) CATPKG 1 - NOTATIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`#`
    When placed immediately after a line break (`\n`), marks the beginning of an option declaration.
    The declaration continues until the next line break (`\n`).

`//`
    Everything from this notation to the next line break (`\n`) is ignored during parsing
    and is considered a comment.

`-`
    Section delimiter symbol (see §2.2).


(2.2) CATPKG 1 - SAP-SECTION DECLARATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A sap-section is a section delimited using `-`.

A sap-section may have different meanings and declarations. The standard declarations
are described below.


(Non-existent section)

`
-
-
`

    This is a non-existent section and returns a NULL value.

    It is still considered a section. Therefore, this is the standard way to skip a section
    when, for any reason, its value should be filled in automatically later.

    A sap-section is considered non-existent if it does not contain a single character.

    For clarification, see "A brief overview of key terms".


(Existing section)

`
-

-
`

    A section is considered to exist if the sap-section contains at least one character,
    including a line break.


(Single-line section)

`
-
Hello world
-
`

    A standard single-line section is considered single-line if its sap-section contains
    exactly one line break character (`\n`).

    For clarification, see "A brief overview of key terms".


(Multiple-line section)

`
-
Hello world 1
Hello world 2
Hello world 3
-
`

    A standard multiple-line section is considered multiple-line if its sap-section contains
    more than one line break character (`\n`).

    For clarification, see "A brief overview of key terms".


(2.3) CATPKG 1 - OPTIONS DECLARATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Options provide a formal mechanism for adding additional values and metadata to CATPKG 1.

An option begins with `#`, followed by the option name:

`
#option_name
`

The option name may be followed by a space and its associated value:

`
#option_name text for option
`

Options are commonly interpreted as separate arguments. Therefore, there is also
an informal convention for representing option values as arguments:

`
#option_name arg1 arg2 arg3
`

Depending on the specification, the value may instead be interpreted as a single
piece of data.


(3) CATPKG 1.0/1.1 SPECIFICATIONS
=======================================================================================================

The CATPKG 1 format defines primitives, templates, notations, and other structural elements.
Minor versions (1.0, 1.1, etc.) define specifications for these primitives and implement
the format for a particular purpose.

This section describes the various specifications of the CATPKG 1 format.

More precisely, a specification defines:

    - SECTION NAMES
    - SECTION DECLARATIONS
    - INTERNAL SECTION SPECIFICATIONS


(3.1) CATPKG 1.0 - PATCH 0
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(3.1.1) SECTION NAMES

    0 - name
        The name of the package.

    1 - version
        The package version.

    2 - architecture
        The processor architecture for which the package is designed.

    3 - description
        A brief description of the package.

    4 - SHA
        The SHA-256 signature of the payload.

    5 - dependency
        Dependencies of the current package.

    7 - options
        Options of the current package. They may serve both as a static description
        of the package and as declarations of actions that need to be performed
        when working with the package.


(3.1.2) SECTION DECLARATIONS

    0 - (Single-line section), (Existing section)

    1 - (Single-line section), (Existing section)

    2 - (Single-line section), (Existing section)

    3 - (Multiple-line section) OR (Single-line section) OR (Non-existent section)

    4 - (Single-line section), (Existing section)

    5 - (Multiple-line section) OR (Single-line section) OR (Non-existent section)

    6 - (Multiple-line section) OR (Single-line section) OR (Non-existent section)


(3.1.3) INTERNAL SECTION SPECIFICATIONS

    (3.1.3.1) OPTIONS - options (see §3.1.1)


(*.1) BUILDER PROTOCOL

`
#prot n value
`

`
#prot n arg0:arg1:arg2
`

    n
        Protocol number (depends on the package manager version).

    value
        A single value passed to the protocol.

    [arg0, arg1, arg2... argI]
        Values passed to the protocol as separate arguments, ordered from 0 to I
        and separated by the colon symbol (`:`).


(*.2) WHERE

`
#WHERE ((<left_operand>) is (<right_operand>))
`

* NOTATIONS

    `<left_operand>` / `<right_operand>`
        Operands that imply the use of a postprocessor to substitute a value
        for the corresponding macro.

    `is`
        A pseudo-operator used to compare operand values.
        The values themselves are treated as text.


(*.2.1) `#WHERE_END`

    A non-independent option that indicates the end of the `#WHERE` option.


    (3.1.3.2) DEPENDENCIES DECLARATION - dependency (see §3.1.1)

`
catpackage_name1
catpackage_name2
catpackage_name3
`

    Package dependencies are written on separate lines.
    Each dependency refers to the PACKAGENAME of the requested package.


    (3.1.3.3) VERSION DECLARATION - version (see §3.1.1)

`
X.Y.Z
`

OR

`
X.Y.Z-release
`


(3.2) CATPKG 1.1 - PATCH 1
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(3.2.1) SECTION NAMES

    0 - options
        Options provide additional metadata associated with sections.

    [1, 2, 3 ... I] - info
        Sections containing options that store information about the corresponding section.


(3.2.2) SECTION DECLARATIONS

    0 - (Multiple-line section) OR (Single-line section), (Existing section)

    [1, 2, 3 ... I] -
        (Multiple-line section) OR (Single-line section), (Existing section)


(3.2.3) INTERNAL SECTION SPECIFICATIONS

    (3.2.3.1) OPTIONS - options (see §3.2.1)


(*.1) USE-OPTIONS

`
#use-options path/to/FILEOPTIONS
`

`
#use-options /absolute/path/to/FILEOPTIONS
`

* NOTATIONS

    `path/to/FILEOPTIONS` / `/absolute/path/to/FILEOPTIONS`
        Path to a file containing options (e.g., POLICY or DEPENDENCIES).


(*.2) REQUIRED

`
#required I=package_name
`

* NOTATIONS

    `I`
        Section index.

    `package_name`
        The name of the package that declares a dependency on section I.


(*.3) PROTECTED

`
#protected I
`

* NOTATIONS

    `I`
        Section index.


    (3.2.3.2) SECTION INFO - info (see §3.2.1)


(*.1) TYPE

`
#type symlink/file/directory
`

* NOTATIONS

    `symlink/file/directory`
        The value must be `symlink`, `directory`, or `file`.


(*.2) PATH

`
#path /path/to/file/or/folder
`

* NOTATIONS

    `/path/to/file/or/folder`
        The path associated with the section.


(*.3) TARGET

`
#target /path/to/target
`

* NOTATIONS

    `/path/to/target`
        The path referenced by the section.


(*.4) SOURCE

`
#source /path/to/source
`

* NOTATIONS

    `/path/to/source`
        The source from which the path was obtained.


(4) GENERAL RULES
=======================================================================================================

To indicate the CATPKG version, specify the version on the first line of the file.

For example:

`
CATPKG 1.0
`

`
CATPKG 1.1
`

`
CATPKG 2.0
`


